# Broad reference is the grammar term for a `this`, `that`, or `which`
# that points at a whole earlier statement instead of at one noun (`This
# means the cache stays warm`, `, which means the pool is empty`). The
# reader has to pick which part of the last sentence the pronoun names.
# The fix names the noun (`This retry means`) or joins the two claims.
#
# `this` counts only before a verb that states a consequence, such as
# `means`, `causes`, or `leads`, with or without `also` or `then`
# between them (`this also means`). `This is` alone also names a field
# or a section (`this is set to True`), so it counts only before
# `because`, `why`, or `how`. `that` counts only before `means`, and it
# also matches a `that` that points at the noun before it (`a value that
# means no value is present`), a miss the rule accepts.
#
# `which` counts only after a comma, and only before `means`, `causes`,
# `leads`, `results`, or the past form of the last three, or before `is
# why`, `is because`, or `is how` (`, which is why the write waits`).
# `, which allows` and its kin stay out, because in about half of the
# sampled hits `which` pointed at the noun before the comma (`the
# mirroring feature, which allows`). The ability rule reports `allows
# you to`, `lets you`, `gives you`, and `makes it possible`, so a guard
# glued to each verb skips those. `helps` is left out, because the
# chatbot rule reports `hope this helps` and the ability rule reports
# `helps to` and `helps you`.
#
# It hit 436 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# pointed at a whole clause.
expr verb [means, meant, ensures, happens, prevents, causes, keeps, works, matters, leads, led, results, resulted, (makes!(( )it)), (allows!(( )[you, users])), (lets!(( )[you, users])), (gives!(( )you))]
expr main [(this @verb), (this [also, then] @verb), (that means), ((,) which [causes, caused, leads, led, results, resulted]), (this [is, was] [because, why, how]), ((,) which means), ((,) which [is, was] [why, because, how])]
let message "Broad reference. Name the noun it points at, or join the two claims."
let description "Flags `this`, `that`, and `which` used as a pronoun for a whole earlier clause."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "This retry means the cache stays warm."
allows "This retry leads to a second write."
allows "This field holds the name."
allows "This change fixes the race."
allows "This lets you pin the version."
allows "Hope this helps."
allows "For a Pod, this is set to True before the sandbox starts."
allows "The flag, which is optional, sets the port."
